# TODO - Tiered native execution engine

## Objective

Build a tiered execution engine instead of choosing between interpretation, AOT,
and JIT. Decode Malbolge into a compact execution IR, simplify that IR through
verified state-graph mathematics, compile demonstrably stable regions to native
machine code before execution, specialize hot or mutation-sensitive regions at
runtime, and deoptimize safely to the interpreter whenever a code-version guard
or speculative assumption fails. The normative VM contract remains the semantic
baseline; native tiers are accelerators of identical observable behavior.

## Acceptance

- Interpreter, AOT, JIT, native cache, and deoptimization share one observable
  VM contract; disabling every native tier produces the same guest behavior.
- Observable state, I/O, termination, and diagnostics match the declared
  semantic profile across positive, boundary, and adversarial fixtures.
- The declared scope contains no unresolved placeholder implementation or
  undocumented workaround required for this objective to function.
- Evidence is durable enough to move this TODO to `docs/todo/completed/` and
  remove the exact TODO heading without losing unfinished intent.

## Evidence

- Expected durable artifact surface: `vm/`, `execution/`, `tests/vm/`,
  `benchmarks/interpreter/`.
- Required evidence: semantic fixtures, state/I/O traces where diagnostic, and
  differential results against independent specification-conformant
  implementations; the historical interpreter is compared only on its documented
  agreement domain.
- Prerequisite completion evidence: `safe-rust-malbolge-vm`,
  `self-modification-state-graph-optimizer`.
- Active foundation: `execution/ir/main.rs` owns portable effect IR v3
  (`EffectOp`, `MemoryLiveIn`, and `RegionEffectProgram`) and re-exports the
  VM-owned `TargetProfileRequirement` without creating a Rust crate boundary.
  State-graph research reprojects verifier traces into this IR,
  rejects tampered profile-ID/fingerprint/version/features/geometry,
  live-in/budget/outcome/effect fields, and preserves
  deterministic interpreter deoptimization on guard miss.
- `vm/src/profile.rs::preflight_runtime_requirement()` consumes an independently
  admitted IR requirement against an explicit runtime capability. Current IR
  produces byte-identical `MALBOLGE-PROFILE-001` text to canonical descriptor
  preflight under `safe-rust-classic`, is admitted by `safe-rust-profiled`, and
  rejects unknown feature IDs explicitly.
- `select_verified_direct_native()` now requires that explicit runtime capability,
  derives exact region memory from the IR, and applies program/profile capacity
  before runtime capability, host validation, or backend construction. `002` then
  `001` precedence is explicit and neither degrades to direct deopt.
- State-applying emitters/verifiers repeat the declared-profile capacity check,
  so bypassing selection cannot promote profile-invalid initial/register halt IR.
- `execution/cache/main.rs` now binds canonical byte-exact IR identity to host
  OS, ISA, backend revision, native ABI revision, and required features. Raw
  untrusted IR remains canonically transportable, but `RegionEffectIdentity` and
  `NativeArtifactKey` reject profile-capacity-inconsistent addressing through
  typed `NativeIdentityError::ProfileCapacity` before hashing or artifact
  construction. `NativeArtifactCache<Value>` adds caller-owned process-local
  lookup/insertion/replacement/removal. Derived digests are excluded from exact
  identity equality; operations prefer the indicated bucket but search all buckets
  when needed, so equal keys with different accelerator digests remain one entry.
  Digest collisions remain distinct because every operation confirms full key
  equality. Cache equality compares exact retained mappings rather than bucket
  layout, so the accelerator is absent from logical store identity. It owns no
  persistence, eviction, synchronization, or semantic admission.
- `execution/native/main.rs` now lowers structurally consistent effect IR to
  deterministic freestanding C23 under an exact `NativeArtifactKey`. All local
  entry/memory/input/output guards precede a collapsed final commit. Pinned
  Clang 22.1.8 emits real untrusted Windows COFF object candidates for both
  x86-64 and AArch64. Safe-Rust structural admission checks ISA, `.text`, entry
  identity, and relocation/symbol closure without trusting LLVM inspection.
  `execution/native/profile_metadata.rs` now solely owns the format-neutral MBPF
  encoding consumed by bootstrap/direct emitters and COFF admission. Direct
  deopt/initial-halt revision-4, halt-observation revision-5, plus halt-fetch,
  non-graphical, and no-operation revision-1 objects must carry exact read-only
  `MBPF` v3
  `.mbprof`
  metadata matching the profile ID/fingerprint, published version, semantic
  features, word trits, profile capacity, and derived `u64` region memory retained
  by their native key; absence or mismatch fails structurally. Bootstrap revision
  2 renders the same canonical payload into its C23 source and structural
  admission requires the section for that revision. Historical revision 1 remains
  admissible without it; pinned Clang cross-ISA compilation is the object-level
  confirmation boundary.
- A direct deopt-only backend now emits canonical x86-64/AArch64 COFF without
  Clang. Complete byte fixtures plus structural validation admit only the exact
  `return guard-miss` programs; opcode mutation fails semantic admission. This is
  the first semantically admitted native artifact and intentionally never applies
  effects.
- `direct-initial-halt` is the first admitted state-applying template. It accepts
  only zero-state/no-I/O/no-memory one-step halt IR, preflights before commit,
  changes only termination on hit, and returns guard miss atomically otherwise.
  Complete object fixtures and x86-64 hit/miss/null execution evidence cover the
  boundary; ARM64 object linkage is also verified.
- `direct-halt-registers` revision 5 extends the same one-step halt contract across
  arbitrary 32-bit `A/C/D` values and full 64-bit input/output counter
  observations while memory/I/O effects remain forbidden. Independently rendered
  495/564-byte x86-64/AArch64 fixtures bind counters above `u32::MAX`; counter,
  opcode, and revision mismatch fail closed. The only commit remains termination.
- `direct-halt-fetch` revision 1 binds one exact graphical code-cell live-in to
  real halt termination. VM-owned positional decode must return `v`; full entry
  observation, memory pointer, `memory_words > C`, exact `memory[C]`, and prior
  termination are guarded before committing only tag `1`. Independently rendered
  535/628-byte objects, x86-64 atomic execution, and AArch64 instruction decoding
  bind the contract.
- `direct-non-graphical` revision 1 is the first exact memory-live-in direct
  template. One VM-classified non-graphical live-in must equal `memory[C]`; full
  entry observation, memory pointer, and `memory_words > C` are guarded before
  dereference, and only termination tag `2` is committed. Independently rendered
  538/631-byte objects, x86-64 hit/live-in/capacity/null execution, and AArch64
  instruction decoding bind the contract.
- `direct-no-operation` revision 1 is the first non-terminal and guest-memory
  writing direct template. VM-owned no-op classification, `XLAT2`, and modular
  pointer successor derive the exact IR exit. Full fetched-cell guards precede a
  single commit of encrypted `memory[C]` plus advanced `C/D`. Independent
  557/658-byte objects bind `77->65`, `5->6`, and `7->8`; x86-64 execution and
  AArch64 instruction decoding prove hit semantics and atomic misses.
- After program/profile/runtime admission, direct-template orchestration
  classifies IR before backend construction: exact zero-register halt selects the
  smallest fast path, other no-live-in halt IR selects the observation-bound
  template, graphical halt fetch and non-graphical termination select terminal
  live-in templates, exact no-op IR selects the memory-writing template, and
  remaining IR selects verified deopt. Unsupported direct host
  formats remain
  explicit at this narrow boundary and post-selection errors never degrade
  silently to fallback.
- `select_preflighted_execution_tier()` adds a product-neutral plan above that
  boundary. Only top-level direct format absence maps to the normative interpreter
  after preflight; `002`, `001`, and backend/emission/admission failures remain
  errors. It performs no cache lookup, executable memory, linking, or invocation.
- `select_cached_preflighted_execution_tier()` adds caller-owned direct reuse under
  explicit `DirectHost`. A private `PreparedDirectTarget` binds specialization to
  one exact key used by lookup and consumed by miss emission, avoiding a second IR
  canonicalization there; state-applying verifiers still reconstruct expected
  identity independently. `VerifiedDirectNativeCache` receives artifacts only
  after semantic admission, reports exact-key `Inserted`/`Hit`, matches uncached
  keys/bytes for all six templates, and reuses one pointer-identical immutable
  `Arc` allocation. Profile and host preflight occur before lookup; `002`, `001`,
  and interpreter selection leave cache cardinality unchanged. Exact-key
  invalidation removes one future reuse entry. Exact-program invalidation first
  constructs canonical region identity, removes every host/backend variant, and
  leaves unrelated regions unchanged; profile-invalid IR fails before mutation.
  Exact-target invalidation removes all regions sharing one complete
  OS/ISA/backend revision/native-ABI/features identity while preserving other
  targets. Outstanding `Arc` plans remain valid and reinsertion restores the same
  key/bytes under new allocations. `Arc` owns immutable sharing only; persistence,
  automatic eviction, synchronization policy, linking, executable memory,
  invocation, and performance remain outside.
- General instruction-specific data-memory/I/O direct selection, cache-aware
  AOT/JIT
  policy beyond verified direct process-local reuse, durable cache
  serialization/storage/eviction, executable-memory policy, invocation, and
  end-to-end tier performance remain unimplemented.
